diff --git a/tests/unit/input/wheel_capability_20261023.md b/tests/unit/input/wheel_capability_20261023.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3f910d43805f3b21e773010201f98506172174a9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/unit/input/wheel_capability_20261023.md
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+# FH2 opened-haptic capability gate
+
+Miho request 409700. Source-only successor of SDK `25379a123c55808f5fd61f13a875b3da0efd06d6`, selected by FH2 app `c760995756e9248e4a623d09ee791e38d722564a`. No app gitlink or delivered binary changed.
+
+## Proved false advertisement and smallest correction
+
+`OnJoystickAddedLocked` caches `X_INPUT_CAPS_FFB_SUPPORTED` before calling `OpenWheelHapticLocked`. That opener can return without a handle when the haptic subsystem is unavailable, the joystick is not haptic, or SDL_OpenHapticFromJoystick fails. Previously `GetDeviceCapabilities` copied the cached flag regardless. Removing a separately bound force device closes its haptic handle while retaining the steering wheel and cached flags, so it also continued reporting FFB after disconnect.
+
+The correction clears only the returned FFB bit when `wheel_->haptic` is null, inside the existing DrainAndLock scope. The cached capability template stays intact so successful reconnect can advertise again. Wheel identity, steering/pedal/shifter fields, unrelated capability bits, gamepad queries and error returns remain unchanged. This matches the existing `HasForceFeedback` opened-handle condition without recursively taking its lock. Other force producers and focus/close safety are unchanged.
+
+The old comment explicitly forced FFB to obtain the title's wheel path even without a host haptic device (FB-2). Consequently this fail-closed candidate may change guest steering-path selection for a non-haptic wheel or a failed host open. It must remain isolated until matched integration qualification; it is not an owner-ready replacement.
+
+`XInputdFFGetDeviceInfo` still reports 16 virtual effect slots and effect calls still complete. The kernel source documents these as guest setup compatibility: leaving setup incomplete formerly caused repeated setup and no rendered frame. Those responses do not report host readiness; the log separately queries HasForceFeedback. Zeroing slot counts or refusing asynchronous completion would conflate virtual guest allocation with host output and could reintroduce that failure. This candidate leaves the compatibility path untouched.
+
+An open handle is the precise capability boundary here, not proof that every effect is supported, created successfully, running, or felt. Existing SDL feature checks and effect creation failures still govern individual effects.
+
+## Focused fixture and executed checks
+
+`wheel_capability_seam.py` extracts the complete production GetDeviceCapabilities body into a fake-device C++ harness, following the existing wheel_focus_seam.py approach. Cases: null output, connected steering without haptics, successful open, split force-device removal/reopen, unrelated flag/input-field preservation, unchanged gamepad path, unknown device with unchanged output, and wheel removal. No SDL, game, device or owner data is accessed. The first no-haptic assertion distinguishes the parent bug.
+
+Executed: Python `--emit-only` generated the harness, and git diff --check passed. **C++ compilation and assertions have not run:** no shared build slot was taken. The later assigned fixture operator can use:
+
+```powershell
+python tests/unit/input/wheel_capability_seam.py --cxx <assigned-cxx> --out <fresh-artifact-directory>
+```
+
+The existing focus fixture does not need changes: no force/focus implementation was edited. Full SDK/app build, actual binary identity and any consumer admission remain separate, with the existing app source/SDK gitlink composition route.
+
+## Physical witness gate
+
+After a separately assigned build and runtime slot, pin the app/SDK and produced pair and observe actual config load, selected SDL steering/force devices and active guest player. An intentionally denied/failed haptic open must not report FFB; successful open and split force-device removal/reconnect must update the queried flag without corrupting steering input or hanging guest setup. Then independently establish supported effect creation, low-force direction/magnitude, and physical stop on focus loss/close plus safe resume. Keep host centering explicitly controlled independently of guest force strength/mode. A capability response, fake fixture, open handle or completed guest call cannot close those physical observations.
+
+No game/UI/device launch, shared slot/lease, build, install, owner settings or accepted force/performance change occurred.
+
+## Endian-wrapper correction after the assigned first build
+
+Miho 410493 later assigned one full app build on source 1170761 / SDK ae7da466. Its integer-stand-in fixture passed, but compiling the real SDL input driver failed at flags `&=`: the actual `X_INPUT_CAPABILITIES.flags` is `be<uint16_t>` (`endian_store`), which has conversion and ordinary assignment but no bitwise compound assignment. Retained failure/custody result: hub `artifacts/fh2-haptic-app-femi-20261023/build-01/result.json`. No new pair or generated checks completed; child quiescence and lease release passed.
+
+This source-only repair replaces compound assignment with the same mask expression assigned normally. The fixture now includes the production `rex/input/input.h`, uses the actual capability structure/endian wrapper and C++23, compares all returned bytes and explicitly checks big-endian flag bytes. No wrapper/operator implementation or force behavior is changed. The older mock's compile pass did not qualify the production type; it is not reused as repair evidence. The corrected fixture has only been emitted, not compiled or run, pending a new assigned slot.
